TOOL=codex
TIMEOUT=1800
CAPTURE_STDOUT=false
CAPTURE_STDOUT_ONLY=false
OUTPUT_FILE=<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.9416.txt
CMD_JSON=["codex","exec","--sandbox","read-only","-C","<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>","--add-dir","<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>","-m","gpt-5.5","-c","projects.\"/Users/zhupanov/larch9\".trust_level=\"trusted\"","-c","model_provider=\"openai-larch-env\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.name=\"OpenAI API (larch env key)\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.base_url=\"https://api.openai.com/v1\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.env_key=\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"","-c","model_providers.openai-larch-env.wire_api=\"responses\"","--output-last-message","<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.9416.txt","--json","--","You are an expert engineer researching this repository and producing an implementation plan for /design Step 2b.\n\nYou may use only side-effect-free repository discovery. Do not write repository files, design tmpdir files, or any other files. Return only the sentinel-delimited response requested below.\n\nDrafting requirements to follow:\n- Prefer minimum necessary change: avoid scope creep, unnecessary complexity, and additions not required for correctness.\n- Read approach-synthesis.txt: if it is exactly NO_SKETCHES, draft from direct codebase/doc inspection without fabricating planning-panel agreement.\n- Read discussion-round1.md when present for scope boundaries and strict constraints.\n- Read design-outline.md only when non-empty and .outline-approved exists; treat Goals, Non-goals, and Surfaces as binding scope.\n- Read brainstorm.md when present as additive ideation context for plan drafting.\n- Use a Files to modify/create section with per-file headings exactly one path each: ### NEW:, ### UPDATED:, or ### REWRITTEN: (at least one ASCII space after ### before the keyword).\n- Include Approach, Edge cases, Failure modes when non-trivial, Testing strategy, optional diff_added/diff_deleted/mechanical_churn trailers, and final diff_lines: <N>. mechanical_churn accepts only true or false; never write a number there.\n- The final plan body must end with a whole-line diff_lines: <N> trailer.\n- Optionally include up to three dynamic plan-review archetypes in a scout block after the plan. The launcher validates, filters, caps, and materializes this block; invalid post-plan scout output is ignored.\n- Scout sentinels inside the summary or plan are fatal format errors. Never put LARCH_SCOUT_* markers in the plan body.\n\nReadability style (trusted):\n# Readability Style\n\n**Consumer**: `/design` prompt surfaces that produce user-facing text, either through an external-agent prompt token or through inline orchestration prose.\n\n**Contract**: this file is the only source of truth for the `/design` readability preamble. External prompt files embed the literal `<READABILITY_STYLE>` token, and assembly code or orchestration expands it to this full file. Inline composition sites read this file before writing user-facing text.\n\n**When to load**: load only when a `/design` step is about to compose user-facing prose or render a prompt that asks another agent to compose such prose. Do not load during setup, pure validation, or byte-stable artifact handling.\n\n---\n\n## Style Axes\n\nWrite in three styles at once:\n\n- **Strunk & White**: use active voice. Omit needless words. Prefer concrete nouns and verbs.\n- **Dyslexia-friendly**: use short sentences. Prefer simple words. Break dense ideas into headings and bullets.\n- **Brevity**: shorter is better. Minimize the total artifact length while preserving meaning.\n- **No em dashes**: never use em dashes; use periods, commas, colons, or semicolons instead.\n- **Hedge vs. direct**: hedge uncertain claims (\"may contain\", \"can fail\") instead of absolutes; keep directives imperative.\n\n## Precision Contract\n\nKeep these byte-stable unless the task explicitly edits them:\n\n- fenced code blocks\n- backticked tokens\n- file paths\n- identifiers\n- flag names\n- `### NEW:` / `### UPDATED:` / `### REWRITTEN:` plan grammar\n- the trailing `diff_lines: <N>` line\n\nDo not rewrite those items for style. Explain around them instead.\n\n## Plan-Drafting Reminders\n\nWhen `/design` Step 2b drafts a plan that adds, removes, or converts\nBash fences in `skills/implement/SKILL.md`:\n\n- List `### UPDATED: scripts/test-implement-fence-shape.sh` in\n  **Files to modify/create**.\n- Note that `EXPECTED_OLD` / `EXPECTED_NEW` may need updates.\n\nThis is a discoverability reminder only. Do not add plan grammar.\n\n## Precedence\n\nWhen rules conflict, use this order:\n\n`code references > meaning > brevity > dyslexia-friendly chunking > Strunk & White micro-rewrites`\n\nApply it directly:\n\n- Preserve code references first.\n- Preserve exact meaning next.\n- Cut words before adding layout.\n- Add headings or bullets only when they improve scanning.\n- Polish grammar last.\n\n## Substitution Token\n\nExternal-agent prompt files MUST embed this literal token:\n\n`<READABILITY_STYLE>`\n\nBefore launch, replace every literal `<READABILITY_STYLE>` token with the full contents of this file.\n\n## Examples\n\nBefore: \"It is recommended that the implementer should consider adding a validation step.\"\n\nAfter: \"Add a validation step.\"\n\nBefore: \"The plan should make sure that users are not able to continue when the file cannot be read.\"\n\nAfter: \"Block the flow when the file cannot be read.\"\n\nBefore: \"In order to avoid potential future confusion, documentation can be updated.\"\n\nAfter: \"Update the docs to avoid confusion.\"\n\nBefore: \"The script performs an operation that checks if the path exists.\"\n\nAfter: \"The script checks that the path exists.\"\n\nBefore: \"This could possibly be out of scope depending on how the team wants to proceed.\"\n\nAfter: \"This may be out of scope.\"\n\nRequired output format:\n[optional]\nLARCH_SUMMARY_BEGIN\nA concise summary for large-plan preview. Omit this whole summary block only when no useful summary is needed.\nLARCH_SUMMARY_END\n[/optional]\nLARCH_PLAN_BEGIN\nFull implementation plan body ending with diff_lines: <N>.\nLARCH_PLAN_END\n[optional]\nLARCH_SCOUT_BEGIN\n{\"archetypes\":[{\"name\":\"slug\",\"focus_area\":\"code-quality|risk-integration|correctness|architecture|security\",\"weight\":1,\"rationale\":\"single-line reason\",\"prompt_body\":\"2-6 sentence focus directive ending with the required citation sentence.\"}]}\nLARCH_SCOUT_END\n[/optional]\n\nOptional advisory status may be included between LARCH_STATUS_BEGIN and LARCH_STATUS_END, but the summary, plan, and optional scout sentinels above are the only parsed contract.\n\nUntrusted feature description:\n<feature_description encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n# [BUG] (URGENT) bg-poll-guard blocks all recovery after premature task-notification, forcing 180+ ps polls and 5-14x main-agent cost inflation\n\n## Summary\n\nDuring a `/design 4636` run, the `design-step3-review.sh` background task fired a premature `&lt;task-notification&gt;` (exit 0) before the review actually completed. The `.bg-wait-active` marker remained in place (the wrapper PID was still alive), so `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` correctly blocked all subsequent TMPDIR-referencing Bash and Read tool calls. Recovery waiters launched as `run_in_background: true` were killed by the harness with exit code 144, leaving foreground `ps -p PID` polling as the only viable wait mechanism. The orchestrator ran approximately 180 foreground `ps` calls over ~70 minutes in a growing 1M-token context window, inflating the Claude main-agent bill to ~$83.91 — 5–14× the typical $7–17 for a 5-round design run.\n\nA secondary latent bug was also found: `marker_step_completed` in `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` checks for `.completed/step-5c-terminal` to release the `design-step5c` guard, but `design-step5c.sh` writes `.completed/step-5c` (no `-terminal` suffix). If step5c ever fires a premature notification, this mismatch would leave the guard permanently active until the wrapper's EXIT trap fires.\n\n## Original report\n\nUser-observed during `/design 4636` (run ID `410AD75F-2779-4242-8F39-67115DD22D8C`):\n\n- `design-step3-review.sh` launched as `run_in_background: true` with `timeout: 21600000`.\n- A `&lt;task-notification&gt;` for task `becmoiftm` fired almost immediately (output file had 1 line — essentially empty) with exit code 0.\n- Checking `.step3-review-result.env` and `.completed/step-3` was blocked by `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` with message: `/design immediate-background wait is active. End the turn and wait for &lt;task-notification&gt;; do not poll progress artifacts.`\n- `ps -p 54056` confirmed the wrapper was still alive (0:00.01 CPU, started 5:08PM, persisted ~70 min).\n- `marker_is_live` kept `.bg-wait-active` active because `kill -0 54056` returned 0.\n- Four recovery waiters launched as `run_in_background: true` with the sanctioned `until [ -f \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.completed/step-3\" ]; do sleep 30; done` form initially showed \"completed exit 0\" (another premature notification) and later resolved to \"failed with exit code 144\" — killed by the task harness.\n- Foreground `ps -p PID` (no TMPDIR reference, not blocked) worked as the only viable polling mechanism.\n- ~180 foreground `ps` checks were issued across ~70 minutes.\n- Final review result: `STEP3_REVIEW_LOOP_STATUS=complete`, 5 rounds, `.completed/step-3` present.\n- Total cost: ~$126.12 (Claude main $83.91, Codex $15.16, Cursor $19.44, Claude subprocess $7.61), 252 424k tokens.\n\n## Reproduction scenario\n\nHard to reproduce on demand (depends on a race in the Claude Code task harness), but the contributing conditions were:\n\n1. Launch `design-step3-review.sh` with `run_in_background: true` at the start of a long 5-round plan review.\n2. Observe `&lt;task-notification&gt;` fire within seconds (output file essentially empty) while the wrapper bash process is still alive (`kill -0 PID` succeeds).\n3. Attempt any Read/Bash tool call that references `$DESIGN_TMPDIR` — blocked by `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh`.\n4. Launch a `run_in_background: true` recovery waiter with the sanctioned `until [ -f \".../step-3\" ]; do sleep 30; done` form — harness kills it (exit 144).\n5. Observe that `ps -p PID` foreground calls are the only unblocked wait mechanism.\n\nLatent `step-5c-terminal` bug reproduced by inspection: `grep \"step-5c\" scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` vs `grep \"step-5c\" skills/design/scripts/design-step5c.sh`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n1. After a background task's `&lt;task-notification&gt;` fires, the orchestrator should be able to verify the step sentinel (e.g., `.completed/step-3`) with a lightweight foreground Bash call without being blocked by `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh`.\n2. OR: the premature notification should never fire while the wrapper PID is still alive.\n3. Recovery waiters should not be killed (exit 144) by the task harness — they are the sanctioned mechanism for handling premature notifications.\n4. `marker_step_completed` for `design-step5c` should match the sentinel that `design-step5c.sh` actually writes.\n\n## Observed behavior\n\n1. `&lt;task-notification&gt;` fires prematurely for `design-step3-review.sh`. The wrapper PID is still alive, so `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` correctly keeps the guard active and blocks all TMPDIR reads/polls.\n2. `run_in_background: true` recovery waiters (`until [ -f \"step-3\" ]; do sleep 30; done`) are killed by the harness (exit 144), making them useless as wait mechanisms.\n3. The only unblocked poll is foreground `ps -p PID`, which does not reference the TMPDIR.\n4. `marker_step_completed` in `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` checks `.completed/step-5c-terminal` at line 84, but `design-step5c.sh` writes `.completed/step-5c` (no `-terminal` suffix) — a mismatch that leaves the guard permanently active if step5c ever fires a premature notification.\n\n## Root cause analysis\n\n**Premature notification (uncertain root cause)**: The Claude Code task harness fires `&lt;task-notification&gt;` before the background bash process exits. The current theory is a harness-level race — either the `run_in_background: true` shell wrapper itself exits 0 after spawning the background script, or the harness fire condition is not \"process exited\" but something earlier. The exact mechanism is unknown without harness source access.\n\n**Recovery waiter killed (exit 144)**: `run_in_background: true` background tasks are killed by the task harness (SIGURG / signal 16, exit 128+16=144) after some condition — possibly after the same task ID receives a prior completion notification, or after a harness-managed timeout shorter than `timeout: 21600000`. The recovery waiter processes do not appear in `ps aux` output from inside the background sandbox (PID namespace isolation), so `kill -0 PID` checks inside the waiter would immediately exit 0, causing `until ! ps -p PID` waiters to also exit immediately.\n\n**`step-5c-terminal` mismatch (definite bug)**: `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` line 84:\n```\ndesign-step5c)  sentinel=\"$dir/.completed/step-5c-terminal\" ;;\n```\nBut `design-step5c.sh` writes `.completed/step-5c` (verified by `ls .completed/` in the session). The `-terminal` suffix does not exist.\n\n**Cost amplification**: Each of ~180 foreground `ps` tool calls in a conversation that had grown to hundreds of turns (252M tokens accumulated) consumed the full context window. At Sonnet 4.6 [1m] pricing, that drives main-agent cost from the typical ~$10 to $83.91.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Premature notification**: Task `becmoiftm` output file had 1 line (empty) when first checked; `ps -p 54056` showed the process alive for 70+ minutes after the notification.\n- **Recovery waiter killed**: Final `&lt;task-notification&gt;` for tasks `bdi537sba`, `bp9jne3ya`, `bqt6wo0gc` all reported `failed with exit code 144` (earlier premature notifications for same tasks showed exit 0).\n- **`step-5c-terminal` mismatch** — hook source:\n  ```\n  # scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh line 84\n  design-step5c)  sentinel=\"$dir/.completed/step-5c-terminal\" ;;\n  ```\n  Session sentinel actually written: `.completed/step-5c` (no `-terminal`).\n- **`bash_is_step3_recovery_waiter` whitelist** (line 241–258) correctly matches the `until` form, but this only prevents the hook from *denying* the tool call — it does not prevent the task harness from killing the background process.\n- **`marker_is_live` PID check** (line 113): `kill -0 \"$pid\"` kept the marker live because PID 54056 was the bash wrapper still running — the logic was correct for this case, it was the premature notification that was the problem.\n- **Cost comparison**: 10 most recent design runs show Claude main-agent costs of $4–17. This run: $83.91 with 252M tokens vs. next highest 190M tokens.\n- **`bg-poll-guard-denials.count`**: value of 2 observed in session TMPDIR, confirming repeated hook denials.\n\n## Affected files\n\n- `scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` — `marker_step_completed` has the `step-5c-terminal` mismatch (line 84); also the premature-notification release logic at line 104 depends on the step sentinel being present, which creates a circular dependency when the sentinel hasn't been written yet.\n- `skills/design/SKILL.md` — sanctioned recovery waiter form (`until [ -f \".../step-3\" ]`) does not survive harness kills (exit 144); the SKILL.md only sanctions one recovery waiter and then requires waiting for the original notification, leaving no fallback.\n- `skills/design/scripts/design-step5c.sh` — writes `.completed/step-5c`; does not write `.completed/step-5c-terminal` expected by the hook.\n\n## Suggested fix(es)\n\n**Fix 1 — `step-5c-terminal` sentinel mismatch (low effort, definite bug)**:\nChange `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` line 84 from:\n```bash\ndesign-step5c)  sentinel=\"$dir/.completed/step-5c-terminal\" ;;\n```\nto:\n```bash\ndesign-step5c)  sentinel=\"$dir/.completed/step-5c\" ;;\n```\nOr align `design-step5c.sh` to write `.completed/step-5c-terminal` before the EXIT trap fires, whichever is the intended contract.\n\n**Fix 2 — Allow sentinel-check foreground probe after notification (medium effort)**:\nAdd a foreground-only allowance path: when the orchestrator has already received a `&lt;task-notification&gt;` for a background task but the step sentinel is still absent, allow a single foreground `[ -f \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.completed/step-3\" ] &amp;&amp; echo YES || echo NO` probe without blocking. The hook could whitelist this specific form (no sleep, no probe verb, just a file-test and echo) independently of `.bg-wait-active`.\n\n**Fix 3 — Document or mitigate recovery-waiter harness kills (low effort)**:\nDocument in SKILL.md that recovery waiters (`run_in_background: true` `until` loops) can be killed by the harness (exit 144) and provide a fallback: after three consecutive killed recovery waiters, permit repeated foreground `[ -f \"...\" ] &amp;&amp; echo DONE || echo WAIT` sentinel checks (no sleep, no TMPDIR-referencing probe verb) as the recovery path, to avoid hundreds of `ps` calls.\n\n**Fix 4 — Investigate premature notification root cause (separate)**:\nUnderstand why `&lt;task-notification&gt;` fires before the background wrapper exits. If the harness fires on \"shell process exited\" and the shell wrapper forks the review script to background before exiting, either: (a) change `design-step3-review.sh` to never fork to background (keep the wrapper alive until children complete), or (b) report to Anthropic.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Does the Claude Code task harness fire `&lt;task-notification&gt;` when the shell process that wraps the `run_in_background` command exits, regardless of whether that process waited for its children? If so, the fix is in `design-step3-review.sh` (never fork children to background and exit).\n- Is exit code 144 from killed recovery waiters specific to a task-harness timeout, or to some other condition (e.g., a previous notification for the same context)?\n- Should `design-step5c.sh` be writing `.completed/step-5c-terminal` (in addition to or instead of `.completed/step-5c`) as the hook-release sentinel contract?\n- Is there a session-level hook or configuration that can set `LARCH_BG_POLL_GUARD_DISABLE=1` as a last-resort escape hatch when the guard becomes permanently stuck after repeated premature notifications?\n\n\n</feature_description>\n\n\nUntrusted approach synthesis:\n<approach_synthesis encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\nNO_SKETCHES\n\n</approach_synthesis>\n\n\nUntrusted discussion round 1:\n<discussion_round1 encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n## Decision 1: Fix scope\n- **Question**: Which of the four suggested fixes in issue #4688 are in-scope?\n- **Resolution**: Fix the definite `step-5c-terminal`→`step-5c` sentinel mismatch (Fix 1), and add a guard-compatible recovery path so a premature `&lt;task-notification&gt;` no longer forces 180+ foreground `ps` polls (Fixes 2+3). Defer the uncertain harness-level premature-notification root cause (Fix 4) — it is not reliably fixable inside larch.\n- **Source**: user\n\n## Decision 2: Recovery mechanism\n- **Question**: How should the guard let the orchestrator cheaply recover after a premature notification when the wrapper PID is still alive and `run_in_background` recovery waiters are killed (exit 144)?\n- **Resolution**: Whitelist a single lightweight, non-sleeping foreground sentinel probe (a `[ -f \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.completed/step-N\" ]`-style file test that echoes DONE/WAIT) past `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` while `.bg-wait-active` is live. The probe is read-only, references only the completion sentinel (never progress/result artifacts), and contains no sleep — so the anti-polling protection is preserved.\n- **Source**: user\n\n## Decision 3: Sentinel-mismatch fix direction\n- **Question**: Fix the mismatch by changing the hook to expect `.completed/step-5c`, or by making `design-step5c.sh` write `.completed/step-5c-terminal`?\n- **Resolution**: Change `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` to expect `.completed/step-5c`, matching what `design-step5c.sh` already writes and the `.completed/step-N` convention used by every other guarded step. Do NOT modify `design-step5c.sh`. (Minimum change.)\n- **Source**: codebase\n\n## Hard constraints\n- The recovery affordance MUST NOT reopen the anti-polling hole the guard exists to close: no whitelist for progress/result artifacts (`.step3-review-result.env`, task-output files), no sleep loops, no repeated progress polling.\n- Keep the change minimal and surgical: `scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` (sentinel fix + recovery whitelist) plus the recovery guidance the SKILL surface relies on. Avoid touching the broader review-loop control flow.\n- Defer Fix 4 (premature-notification root cause) — harness-level and uncertain; candidate for an OOS follow-up / upstream report.\n\n</discussion_round1>\n\n\nUntrusted approved design outline:\n<design_outline encoding=\"literal-redacted\">\n## Proposed Design Outline\n\n### Goals\n- Fix the definite `step-5c-terminal` sentinel mismatch in `scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` so the `design-step5c` guard releases on the sentinel `design-step5c.sh` actually writes (`.completed/step-5c`).\n- Give the orchestrator a sanctioned, guard-permitted foreground way to check `.completed/step-N` after a premature `&lt;task-notification&gt;`, so recovery stops spiraling into 180+ context-heavy `ps`/poll calls.\n- Add regression tests proving the probe is allowed while real progress-polling stays denied.\n\n### Non-goals\n- The premature-notification harness root cause (Fix 4) — not reliably fixable in larch; candidate OOS / upstream report.\n- Reworking review-loop control flow, the `.bg-wait-active` marker schema, or `design-step3-review.sh` fork behavior.\n- Changing `design-step5c.sh` (align the hook to it, not the reverse).\n\n### Approach sketch\n- `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh`: change the `design-step5c)` case from `.completed/step-5c-terminal` to `.completed/step-5c`.\n- `hook-bg-poll-guard.sh`: add a strict early-allow for a single non-sleeping foreground sentinel probe (`[ -f \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.completed/step-N\" ]` or `test -f …`, optional `&amp;&amp; echo … || echo …` echo-only tail) limited to the `.completed/step-{3,5c,final-summary}` sentinels — mirrors `bash_is_step3_recovery_waiter`, rejects sleep/loops/probe-verbs/result-artifacts.\n- `skills/design/SKILL.md`: document that foreground probe as the sanctioned recovery after a premature notification or a killed recovery waiter (one check, then end the turn).\n\n### Surfaces in scope\n- `scripts/hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` (+ sibling `hook-bg-poll-guard.md`)\n- `scripts/test-hook-bg-poll-guard.sh` (+ sibling `.md`)\n- `skills/design/SKILL.md` recovery-guidance prose\n\n### Open questions\n- Cover the probe whitelist for all three guarded steps (step-3, step-5c, final-summary) or only step-3? (Lean: all three — identical failure mode.)\n\n</design_outline>\n\n"]
OUTER_LAUNCHER=agent launch-codex-exec
OUTER_LAUNCHER_PROMPT_FILE=<TMPDIR>/step2b-codex-raw.9416.txt.prompt
OUTER_LAUNCHER_WORKDIR=<OPERATOR_REPO_PATH>
OUTER_LAUNCHER_KIND=codex-exec
OUTER_LAUNCHER_SANDBOX=read-only
OUTER_LAUNCHER_WITH_EFFORT=false
OUTER_LAUNCHER_USAGE_LABEL=codex_plan_draft
OUTER_LAUNCHER_TIMING_KIND=codex-plan-draft
OUTER_LAUNCHER_ADD_DIRS_JSON=["/Users/zhupanov/larch9"]
